{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>\"When you take the sum of the children of Israel\"</b> — The Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Moses: when you take the sum of the children of Israel by head-count, let them give a ransom for the soul, a half-shekel. And He said to him (Exodus 30:16), \"And you shall take the silver of the atonement money\" — the aforementioned — \"and you shall give it for the service of the Tent of Meeting.\" From this Moses would learn that he must count them now, and so he did, as it says (Exodus 38:25), \"And the silver of those who were counted of the congregation, a hundred talents…\" And there was no need to elaborate and say, \"And now take their sum and give the silver for the service of the Tent of Meeting,\" for the matter is self-evident — that he should count them now. Therefore He subsumed the commandment under the formula: \"When you take their sum, you shall do thus,\" so that it would enter the general rule every time he counts them.<br><br>And it appears to me that He did not require him now to go into their tents and count them as would be done in the Book of Numbers (see Rashi there, Numbers 3:15). Rather, he was to do as our Rabbis say (Shekalim 1:1) concerning the shekels for the offerings: He commanded them that every person who knows himself to be twenty years old and upward shall give this amount, and they would bring him the ransom as a free-will offering together with all the rest of the free-will offering, morning by morning. Therefore He said only, \"And you shall give the silver of the atonement money\" — meaning: I have commanded you that when you count them they shall give their ransom, and now they shall give the ransom of their own accord, and you shall give it for the service of the Tent of Meeting. And therefore there was no need now for Aaron and the princes to be with him. And there is no objection from \"of every one who passes over to those who are counted\" (Exodus 30:13–14), for its proper meaning is: those who pass over to be counted. And because it was not specified here whether this is a commandment for all generations or only for that time in the wilderness, David erred and counted them without shekels (II Samuel 24:4–9), and the plague came upon them, and he confessed it: \"And David said to HaShem, 'I have sinned greatly in what I have done'\" (ibid. v. 10). And our Rabbis (Megillah 29b) expounded from here three contributions from the multiplicity of the verses, and so it appears from what Scripture says (II Chronicles 24:6): \"Why have you not required of the Levites to bring from Judah and from Jerusalem the offering of Moses the servant of HaShem, and of the congregation of Israel, for the Tent of the Testimony?\" — it appears from this that the offering of Moses is a commandment for generations, to bring it for the repair of the House even without counting them, and likewise for the offerings, according to the words of the Sages (Megillah 29b). And so it is written (Nehemiah 10:33–34): \"And we have laid obligations upon ourselves, to give upon ourselves a third of a shekel yearly for the service of the house of our Elohim, for the showbread, and for the continual meal-offering, and for the continual burnt-offering, of the Sabbaths, of the new moons, for the appointed seasons, and for the holy things, and for the sin-offerings to make atonement for Israel, and for all the work of the house of our Elohim.\" From here it is explicit that they would bring shekels every year for the offerings and for the repair of the House.<br><br>And it says \"a third of a shekel\" because in the days of Ezra they added to it, and a third of a shekel was ten gerah. And in tractate Shekalim (2:3) we learned: when Israel came up from the exile they would weigh darkonot; they reverted to weighing selas; they reverted to weighing tevain; and they sought to weigh dinarim. And its meaning is: when they came up from the exile, because the repair of the House required great expenditures, they would weigh darkonot, which are larger than selas, and each person would weigh one darkomon. They reverted to weighing whole selas; they reverted to weighing tevain — and it is explicit in the Jerusalem Talmud (ibid.) that this is half a sela; and they sought to weigh dinarim, but they did not accept it from them — for the public may add and give more than a half-shekel, provided that the hand of all of them is equal, as we learned in the Mishnah of tractate Shekalim. Nevertheless, no person is permitted to diminish and give less than a half-shekel, neither an individual nor the public, for the ransom of the soul is not less than that, as it is written, \"This they shall give\" (Exodus 30:13), etc. And in the Jerusalem Talmud (ibid.) they said regarding \"a third of a shekel\": from here, that a person is liable for the obligation of shekels three times a year; from here, that they do not impose upon the public more than three times a year. And Rashi wrote (on Exodus 30:15): because He alluded here to three contributions — one is the contribution for the sockets, for they were counted when they began with the free-will offering for the Tabernacle after Yom ha-Kippurim in the first year, and each gave a half-shekel, and it came to a hundred talents, as it says (Exodus 38:25), \"And the silver of those who were counted of the congregation, a hundred talents,\" etc. And the second was also by means of a count, counted from the time the Tabernacle was erected — this is the count spoken of in the Book of Numbers (Numbers 1:1), on the first day of the second month in the second year — and each gave a half-shekel to purchase from them the public offerings.<br><br>And if you will say: is it possible that in both counts Israel were equal — six hundred thousand and three thousand and five hundred and fifty? For were they not two years apart, and it is impossible that there were none who at the time of the first count were nineteen years old and then became twenty? The answer to this: with regard to the years of the persons they were counted in one year, but with regard to the reckoning of the Exodus it was two years, for the Exodus is counted from Nisan, and the years of persons are counted according to the reckoning of the years of the world, which begin from Tishrei. Thus both counts fell in one year: one count in Tishrei after Yom ha-Kippurim, when the Holy One, blessed be He, was reconciled to Israel and they were commanded concerning the Tabernacle, and the second on the first of Iyar (Numbers Rabbah 1:10). All these are the words of the Rav.<br><br>And I am astonished: how is it possible that there should be so great a congregation and not die in it over half a year by the hundreds and thousands? For according to the Rav's words, they stood for seven months and not one person died — yet it is written (Numbers 9:6), \"And there were certain men who were defiled by the dead body of a man.\" And a further difficulty for me: the count of the years of persons is not according to the reckoning of the years of the world from Tishrei, but it is from moment to moment from the day of birth. Therefore it says of them \"twenty years old and upward\" (Exodus 30:14), that they should be complete years for them. And so in all the counts of the Torah concerning the years of a person, they are counted from moment to moment, as they said in tractate Arakhin (18b): the \"year\" mentioned in connection with consecrated things, and in the houses of walled cities, and the two years in a field of inheritance, and the six years of a Hebrew slave, and in the son and in the daughter — all are from moment to moment. In consecrated things, how do we know this? The verse says, \"a lamb in its first year\" (Leviticus 12:6) — its year, and not the year of the world's reckoning, etc. And they explain there that the reason for the son and the daughter is — Rav Gidel said: for arakhin (valuations). And the reason is that concerning them it is written \"and upward,\" and in all the wilderness counts \"and upward\" is also written concerning them, and the wilderness count is like the arakhin count in every respect, as it says in Bava Batra (121a): we learn \"and upward\" and \"and upward\" from arakhin. If so, all who were born from Tishrei until Iyar completed a year in between, and the people multiplied greatly between the two counts.<br><br>But it is more correct to say that this is simply what happened: Israel at the time of the first count were 603,550, and many of them died during the seven months as is the way of the world, and those who were turning twenty completed their year from Tishrei to Iyar, and it happened that those who completed were equal in number to those who died. But in my opinion, the equal numbering of these two counts is no question at all, for in the first count the tribe of Levi was counted with them, since it had not yet been chosen and had not gone out from the general body of the people, while in the second count it was said to him: \"Only the tribe of Levi you shall not count, and their sum you shall not take\" (Numbers 1:49). And those who were completing their year, who became twenty between the two counts, were close to twenty thousand. And this is a clear matter — for from the fact that in the second count it was necessary to say, \"Only the tribe of Levi you shall not count, and their sum you shall not take,\" there is proof that it was counted with them until then, and now that tribe was chosen and counted by itself to be a legion for the King. And behold, Israel when they went out from Egypt were about six hundred thousand men on foot (Exodus 12:37) — not exactly six hundred thousand — and some of them died up to that count, and they multiplied through those completing their years. And perhaps \"the men\" (הַגְּבָרִים) are not those of twenty years, but everyone called a man, from thirteen years and upward, is included — for it is only to exclude the women and the little children, as it says, \"besides little ones\" (לְבַד מִטָּף).<br><br>And so it appears to me that the three contributions alluded to here — the first, for the public offerings — is not by means of the count mentioned in the Book of Numbers, as the Rav says, for there it says, \"Only the tribe of Levi you shall not count,\" but the shekels for the offerings — Levites are obligated in them according to all opinions, and according to the Sages, priests as well, and so is the halakhah, as is explicit in tractate Shekalim (1:4) and elsewhere. For the shekels for the offerings are not from twenty years old and upward, but rather from when one grows two hairs he is obligated to give, and so it is explicit there. Rather, Scripture commanded (Exodus 30:12–14) that they bring for the work of the Tabernacle a contribution of a half-shekel from all who pass over to the counted, from twenty years old and upward. And He alluded: \"The rich shall not increase, and the poor shall not diminish, from half a shekel, to make atonement for your souls\" (v. 15) — that everyone who requires atonement, who has reached every obligation of the commandments, shall bring another half-shekel for the offerings."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The abbreviation יוה\"כ is resolved as Yom ha-Kippurim based on context; it appears twice in this passage."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:23.229589030Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}